      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve $\\left\\lfloor -\\frac{5}{3}\\right\\rfloor$, start by understanding the floor function. The floor function, denoted as $\\left\\lfloor x \\right\\rfloor$, gives the greatest integer less than or equal to $x$. So, $\\left\\lfloor -\\frac{5}{3} \\right\\rfloor$ means the greatest integer less than or equal to $-\\frac{5}{3}$.\n\n1. First, estimate the value of $-\\frac{5}{3}$:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{5}{3} = -1.\\overline{6}\n   \\]\n   This means $-\\frac{5}{3}$ is between $-2$ and $0$. \n\n2. Since we need the greatest integer less than or equal to $-\\frac{5}{3}$, we consider:\n   \\[\n   -2 < -\\frac{5}{3} < -1\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the greatest integer less than or equal to $-\\frac{5}{3}$ is $-2$.\n\nHence, $\\left\\lfloor -\\frac{5}{3} \\right\\rfloor = -2$.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
